A macro call that spans several lines is a declaration, so the items inside `cfg_if!` or `thread_local!` don't leave a hole in the outline: its line prints the macro's path and `!`, and its line range starts at its first leading annotation (the doc comment above `bitflags!`) and takes in a closing `;`. Nothing inside a call is listed, since the grammar gives its arguments as a token tree. The one-line `include!` hides no lines and isn't listed. Inside an inline module, a multi-line call is listed one level deeper, like the module's other items.
== args
input/macros.rs
== file input/macros.rs
cfg_if::cfg_if! {
    if #[cfg(unix)] {
        pub fn unix_only() {}
    }
}

/// Flags of a file.
#[derive(Debug)]
bitflags! {
    pub struct Mode: u8 {
        const READ = 1;
    }
}

include!("generated.rs");

impl_ops!(
    Meters,
    Feet
);

mod platform {
    thread_local! {
        static COUNTER: Cell<u32> = Cell::new(0);
    }
    fn reset() {}
}
